  "subtitle": "The first portable electronic data bank — a calculator you type names into, one 8-segment character at a time",
  "why_scout_cares": "Before the Sharp Wizard and the Psion organiser, before anyone coined 'personal digital assistant,' Toshiba made a pocket calculator that could also remember. The Memo Note 30 is the prototypical electronic data bank: a calculator chassis with no full keyboard, where you spell out names and phone numbers character-by-character through the shared 8-key* digit pad, cycling letters onto a cramped 8-segment alphanumeric display, and store them into 30 fixed slots. Each slot holds six letters and eight numerals — 256 bytes you could carry in a shirt pocket. It is the rough, cramped pre-keyboard ancestor of the organizer, and the friction it encodes — typing 'DAVE' one segmented glyph at a time, reading it back off a segmented screen — is precisely the interface problem the later machines dissolved. Nine years ahead of TI's first Mini Data Bank, and sold in America as Radio Shack's EC-4002. Nothing else in the museum shows an electronic memory being fed through a calculator's single shared pad; it belongs in the collection as the ur-organizer.",
  "search_terms": ["Toshiba LC-836MN", "Memo Note 30", "electronic databank 1978", "Toshiba Memo Note", "Radio Shack EC-4002", "Canon LC-MEMO", "personal data bank calculator"],
